hing_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"The rule I found is that each cell of colour 1 in the input becomes the centre of a 3×3 square. Every background (0) cell inside that square is painted 1. Cells that already hold another colour keep it.\n\n**How the evidence supports this**\n- The only change in any pair is 0→1. All other colours keep their exact counts.\n- Pair 1 has 1s at (2,3), (3,7), (9,4), (12,3) and (13,10).\n  - The changed cells are exactly the zero cells in the 3×3 boxes around those 1s.\n  - Around (2,3), rows 1–3 and columns 2–4 gain 1s at every former 0. The 6 at (3,4) and the 4 at (2,5), which sits just outside the box, are untouched.\n  - Around (9,4), only (8,4) and (10,4) change, because the rest of that box is already coloured.\n- Pair 2 behaves the same way, and so does pair 3. In pair 3 the box around (8,6) keeps its 8 at (9,5), and the boxes around (3,1)/(4,1) and (2,13)/(3,13) are filled the same way.\n- I did not run this check on the grids. My spot checks of the listed changed cells all agree, and the program is accepted only if it reproduces every train output exactly.\n\n**Construction**\n1. Find all cells of colour 1 in the input. Using the input, not the growing output, means newly painted 1s never spawn squares of their own.\n2. For each seed, clip its 3×3 box to the grid. Set every 0 inside it to 1, then emit that stage.\n3. Emit the final grid and return it.\n\n**On the test input** the seeds are the 1s at (2,10), (3,1), (4,5), (7,3), (8,8), (11,4) and (13,8), (13,13). Each one gets its surrounding zeros filled with 1. Boxes on the bottom row, such as the one around (13,13), are clipped at the grid border.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid).copy()\n    bg = 0\n    H, W = g.shape\n    seeds = list(zip(*np.where(np.array(grid) == 1)))\n    for (y, x) in seeds:\n        y0, y1 = max(0, y - 1), min(H - 1, y + 1)\n        x0, x1 = max(0, x - 1), min(W - 1, x + 1)\n        changed = False\n        for yy in range(y0, y1 + 1):\n            for xx in range(x0, x1 + 1):\n                if g[yy, xx] == bg:\n                    g[yy, xx] = 1\n                    changed = True\n        if changed:\n            emit(g, f\"Fill the empty cells of the 3x3 square centred on the 1 at ({y},{x}) with colour 1, keeping existing coloured cells.\")\n    emit(g, \"Final grid: every 1 is surrounded by a 3x3 square of 1s wherever the background was empty.\")\n    return g\n","candidate_tactics":{"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"All other colours keep their exact counts."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"c1a5368f1e4db92019a1abbede504e755ce130282df380fa2733d63bef0e8169","task_sha256":"9e9d174f9022fc56df64330c4e430d82be6bd88fdb580b53785ee3575085a0d0","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
